{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/823674-how-do-changelings-do-countermagic-against-mages","id":823674,"title":"how do changelings do countermagic against mages.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Changeling: The Dreaming"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-02-04T13:47:25-08:00","id":823674,"title":"how do changelings do countermagic against mages.","content":"i remember i saw a rule in on book but i just can't find it...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-04T14:37:12-08:00","id":823737,"title":"","content":"Book of lost dreams p. 21:<br /> <br /> To resist mage magick a changeling player must roll her Glamour against a difficulty of the highest Sphere rating used in the effect + 4. If successfull, the changeling receives a point of temporary Banality for having removed some small bit of magick from the world.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-04T18:34:09-08:00","id":823912,"title":"","content":"And in M20, there's another system (Wits + Occult to reduce the mage's successes). The original rule makes more sense, though.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-04T19:46:16-08:00","id":823963,"title":"","content":"M20's system is a bit more more than that. It would be Wits + Occult capped at Glamour, diff 7 (which is pretty similar in application).<br /> <br /> You can actually combine the Book of Lost Dreams version for non-basic countermagic options like the Anti-magic effect which makes more sense as generating Banality (and perhaps could use Banality instead of Glamour). The M20 nightfolk basic countermagic requires an action and decent Glamour and Wits + Occult, is weaving fae magic instead of removing dynamic magic and thus wouldn't cost a Banality, while a changeling could summon up Banality to reduce that spell directly but get the temporary banality knock.<br /> <br /> Seems like more interesting their an either or approach of picking from the existing systems.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-05T09:37:24-08:00","id":824379,"title":"","content":"I always thought using Glamour to resist a Mage's magic but then suffering Banality was punitive to the detriment of the fluff. Powerful faeries scoffing at mortal magics by exerting their own glamourous might are asserting magic in the world, not diminishing it. Resisting Mage powers with BANALITY - that is, asserting the consensus and treating themselves as Witnesses to a Vulgar effect - would be worthy of Banality, sure. But not resisting with Glamour.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-05T20:44:50-08:00","id":824790,"title":"","content":"Yeah, Banality should only really be gained when a changeling *disbelieves* in magic. Otherwise, the system should be as Heavy Arms suggests.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-26T20:53:09-08:00","id":837905,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Segev</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/823674-how-do-changelings-do-countermagic-against-mages?p=824379#post824379\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I always thought using Glamour to resist a Mage's magic but then suffering Banality was punitive to the detriment of the fluff. Powerful faeries scoffing at mortal magics by exerting their own glamourous might are asserting magic in the world, not diminishing it. Resisting Mage powers with BANALITY - that is, asserting the consensus and treating themselves as Witnesses to a Vulgar effect - would be worthy of Banality, sure. But not resisting with Glamour.</div> </div> </div> </div>I disagree, as the Changeling is less asserting their own Will on the world, a more negating the Will of the Mage. They are taking a stance against Magick as a force in Reality. And that's at cross-purposes to a Changeling's interests, even if getting affected by hostile Magick would also run counter to their interests.<br /> <br /> There's another point I'd like to address: using Glamour vs. Banality as the stat to roll for countermagic? Why not both?<br /> <br /> This comes down to what kind of Magick is being used against the Changeling, and there are two potential divisions on this: Technocrat Vs. Mystical, or Coincidental Vs. Vulgar.<br /> <br /> The first has it that the Changeling rolls Glamour to resist Technocrat magick, and Banality to resist Tradition/Craft magick. This would mean that it would behoove a Changeling to stock up on Banality in order to fight the mystically inclined, but purge it against Technocrats (which is handy, as Technocrats are often though not always the bigger threats to Changelings). But &quot;Technocrat&quot; is a largely arbitrary label. Technocratic magick runs on the same metaphysics (or meta-metaphysics) that Tradition magick, and any member of the Conventions would balk at the idea of Science being lacking in ability to inspire wonder and creativity. That's unmutal, reductive thinking, and ignores that even the Technocracy laments the slow apathy (read Banality) of humanity, in any form.<br /> <br /> Alternatively, you have resisting Coincidental magick vs. Vulgar magick, with Glamour and Banality respectively. Then the conflict becomes one of countering magick that Affirms the Banal Consensus, versus countering magick that violates it. And since Technocrats endeavor to stay coincidental as much as humanly possible, Changelings will still find themselves resisting Technocrat Procedures with Glamour 99% of the time. On the other hand, Tradition and Craft Mages also try to remain coincidental, simply to avoid being blown up/cursed/haunted/dragged to an otherworldly hell. Really, the most mileage a high-Banality Changeling could get with countermagic would be against the Taftani. And how often do Changelings encounter carpet-riding desert magicians anyway?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-29T08:19:14-08:00","id":839187,"title":"","content":"Unless the case is being made that Glamour and Magic (the Mage version) are both about reality-warping, rather than working &quot;with&quot; it (in which case Mages should be perhaps a little more interested in Arcadia), there is nothing in the Changelings' interest in Magic as Mages work it.<br /> <br /> I think it's...just not well thought-out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-01T18:47:00-08:00","id":840307,"title":"","content":"Well...the reasoning behind the original ability of Changelings to have innate Countermagic was that both powers (True Magick and Glamour) come from &quot;the same source&quot;. <br /> <br /> This has some sense within Changeling (everything it's related to the Dreaming. Mages have learned to create powerful dreams that change reality), but not much from Mage's point of view (with its distinction of Dynamic and Static magick)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-02T01:46:16-08:00","id":840489,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/823674-how-do-changelings-do-countermagic-against-mages?p=840307#post840307\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well...the reasoning behind the original ability of Changelings to have innate Countermagic was that both powers (True Magick and Glamour) come from &amp;quot;the same source&amp;quot;. <br /> <br /> This has some sense within Changeling (everything it's related to the Dreaming. Mages have learned to create powerful dreams that change reality), but not much from Mage's point of view (with its distinction of Dynamic and Static magick)</div> </div> </div> </div>In Mage terms, mages enact dynamic magic by applying their will to the universe. However, out of character, we know that they're actually able to enact magic because they have awakened Avatars which are part of an objective reality in which magic does exist. There's a reason, too, why mages sometimes refer to other supernatural beings as awakened (in a looser sense of the term than the sense they use to refer to themselves): because these beings are connected to their own kinds of Avatars, which also engage with that objective reality to work the magic it contains. Werewolves (or their Avatars) are connected to Gaia, which binds them to the universe in certain ways and limits their magic, but also makes them fit into the consensus. That's why a werewolf can never also become a mage--their Avatar is forever bound up in other matters. <br /> <br /> If you also understand that Glamour is just one type of Quintessence, albeit in a less refined form, you realise that the True Fae are pretty much comprised of Quintessence, and so a changeling is a human soul with a fragment of something magical attached to it (which sounds rather like mages, in fact...).<br /> <br /> The Dreaming, whether as an expression of the collective unconscious/sleeping Avatar(s) of humanity or as a particular kind of nonsentient greater spirit like Gaia, exerts itself upon the universe when a changeling successfully casts a cantrip. It acts like an uber-Avatar.<br /> <br /> So you can draw parallels between a mage's awakened Avatar and a changeling's awakened faerie soul. They are both spirit fragments that allow a being to work magic. Arguably, the fae soul is a kind of undeveloped Avatar, or an Avatar that has evolved differently, to adapt to different needs.<br /> <br /> Mages like to think they're special, and that their power comes from some innate quality of their own, but the truth is that it's the supernatural fragment attached to their souls which allows them to do magic (whether that's part of God or the universe or whatever). It's actually an external thing attached to them, which channels their will. Likewise, a changeling is empowered in much the same way, even though their Avatars don't give them the same free rein.<br /> <br /> I'm also not convinced faerie magic truly is static. Especially with the new rules for Unleashing, the fae have much more flexibility than they once had. The problem is that they're always working at one step removed from the source of magic: they beseech the Dreaming to enact the magic, rather than doing it directly themselves (presumably because their 'Avatars' are part of, or fused with, the Dreaming). This is what limits their Arts.<br /> <br /> tl;dr: Most beings in the WoD appear to have something resembling an Avatar, but it manifests in different ways, and is bound up in different activities. Changelings and mages aren't that dissimilar as a result.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-18T08:58:04-07:00","id":849297,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>adambeyoncelowe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/823674-how-do-changelings-do-countermagic-against-mages?p=840489#post840489\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> In Mage terms, mages enact dynamic magic by applying their will to the universe. However, out of character, we know that they're actually able to enact magic because they have awakened Avatars which are part of an objective reality in which magic does exist. There's a reason, too, why mages sometimes refer to other supernatural beings as awakened (in a looser sense of the term than the sense they use to refer to themselves): because these beings are connected to their own kinds of Avatars, which also engage with that objective reality to work the magic it contains. Werewolves (or their Avatars) are connected to Gaia, which binds them to the universe in certain ways and limits their magic, but also makes them fit into the consensus. That's why a werewolf can never also become a mage--their Avatar is forever bound up in other matters.<br /> <br /> If you also understand that Glamour is just one type of Quintessence, albeit in a less refined form, you realise that the True Fae are pretty much comprised of Quintessence, and so a changeling is a human soul with a fragment of something magical attached to it (which sounds rather like mages, in fact...).<br /> <br /> The Dreaming, whether as an expression of the collective unconscious/sleeping Avatar(s) of humanity or as a particular kind of nonsentient greater spirit like Gaia, exerts itself upon the universe when a changeling successfully casts a cantrip. It acts like an uber-Avatar.<br /> <br /> So you can draw parallels between a mage's awakened Avatar and a changeling's awakened faerie soul. They are both spirit fragments that allow a being to work magic. Arguably, the fae soul is a kind of undeveloped Avatar, or an Avatar that has evolved differently, to adapt to different needs.<br /> <br /> Mages like to think they're special, and that their power comes from some innate quality of their own, but the truth is that it's the supernatural fragment attached to their souls which allows them to do magic (whether that's part of God or the universe or whatever). It's actually an external thing attached to them, which channels their will. Likewise, a changeling is empowered in much the same way, even though their Avatars don't give them the same free rein.<br /> <br /> I'm also not convinced faerie magic truly is static. Especially with the new rules for Unleashing, the fae have much more flexibility than they once had. The problem is that they're always working at one step removed from the source of magic: they beseech the Dreaming to enact the magic, rather than doing it directly themselves (presumably because their 'Avatars' are part of, or fused with, the Dreaming). This is what limits their Arts.<br /> <br /> tl;dr: Most beings in the WoD appear to have something resembling an Avatar, but it manifests in different ways, and is bound up in different activities. Changelings and mages aren't that dissimilar as a result.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> by &quot;new rules for unleashing&quot; i assume you refer to dark ages fae right? this is not really a retcon however. even changeling core rule book when mentioning the arts says something like&quot; the power of the true fae is greaterr&quot;<br /> <br /> presumably the fact that arcadia has been cut off from the world dampens their magic.<br /> <br /> which raises an interesting question. is the return of arcadia a good thing for humanity. for the changelings might become the terrible fair folk of old..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-18T14:39:54-07:00","id":849459,"title":"","content":"I believe the reference is to Unleashing being included in C20.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-18T14:56:57-07:00","id":849469,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/823674-how-do-changelings-do-countermagic-against-mages?p=849459#post849459\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I believe the reference is to Unleashing being included in C20.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> no such book has been released. did you read it in a blog or something like that?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-18T16:37:07-07:00","id":849505,"title":"","content":"It's in update #17 for the Kickstarter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}